  "content": {
    "id": "v2-7aed5b-p9-practice-slot-1-lower-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Explain why multiplying both sides of $\\dfrac{3}{x-2}>1$ by $x-2$ may require the inequality sign to reverse, but multiplying by $(x-2)^2$ never does.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x-2$ can be positive or negative, so the direction is unknown. $(x-2)^2>0$ for every $x\\ne2$.",
      "worked": "The expression is defined for $x\\ne2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x>2:&\\ x-2>0,\\text{ so the sign stays}\\\\x<2:&\\ x-2<0,\\text{ so the sign reverses}\\end{align*}$$\nWe do not know which case applies, so we cannot choose the direction. But $(x-2)^2>0$ for all $x\\ne2$, so multiplying by it never reverses the inequality sign.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
